            <div xml:id="d737">

                <pb facs="0986" n="872" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg872"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">863.4061 All Quiet/13: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Austria</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stockton</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Vienna</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-11-05T17:00:00+01:00">November 5, 1931—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 5—2:40 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>122. I have been urging the Austrian Government to permit the showing of the film
                    “All Quiet on the Western Front” in Austria since the exhibition of the film has
                    been allowed in Germany. In spite of the determined opposition of the Minister
                    of the Interior, Winkler, the question was at last brought before a ministerial
                    council on November 3. In view of the present unrest in Austria, especially
                    among the National Socialists, it was decided not to remove the ban on the film
                    for the present as disturbances might follow. I was informed by the Foreign
                    Office, however, that this decision was not meant to suggest that the picture
                    might not be released at some time in the future.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stockton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
